From 7c237a16f5fe5855df52631298191baa72b7da15 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: srymsjackson <jstrazzo22@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:35:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add Twilio signature replay-window protection (last item,
 hardening plan)

Twilio's webhook signature scheme (X-Twilio-Signature, HMAC-SHA1 over URL +
sorted params) has no nonce or timestamp -- the same URL and params always
produce the same valid signature, forever. A request captured once (a
compromised proxy, a debugging tool that logged a raw body, a bug that
echoed one back) stays replayable indefinitely: resending the exact same
bytes months later still passes validate().

app/security.py's verify_twilio_signature() now also checks the request
hasn't been seen before outside a 10-minute window. Deliberately permissive
in one direction: an exact-duplicate signed request seen again *within* the
window is silently allowed, not rejected, because Twilio itself retries a
webhook that times out or returns 5xx with the identical signed request.
Breaking that would mean flaky network conditions start dropping real
calls -- worse than the vulnerability being fixed. Only a repeat *outside*
the window is treated as a replay and rejected with 403.

Request identity is a SHA-256 hash of the URL plus sorted params, so two
different turns of the same call (same CallSid, different SpeechResult) or
the same params sent to two different routes are correctly treated as
different requests, not replays of each other.

Storage is in-process memory (a module-level dict, pruned on each check),
matching the same tradeoff the app already makes for CALL_STATE in
app/routes/voice.py -- fine for the single Railway instance this runs on,
wouldn't survive a restart or share state across horizontally-scaled
instances. Flagged in the code as the natural next step alongside
CALL_STATE's own Redis-backed-store TODO, not duplicated as a new one.

Tests: tests/test_twilio_replay_window.py (6 new tests) -- duplicate within
the window allowed twice, duplicate outside the window rejected, one-second
boundary case still allowed, different params/different routes correctly
treated as independent, and a direct test of the pruning logic (HTTP-level
tests alone can't see whether old entries actually get dropped, and an
unbounded dict is a slow memory leak that wouldn't otherwise show up as a
failure). Also added a global _reset_twilio_replay_cache autouse fixture in
tests/conftest.py, mirroring the existing rate-limiter reset fixture --
the module-level cache is shared across the whole test session and several
existing test files send Twilio-signed requests with identical dummy
params, so without a reset a later test's "first" request could coincide
with an earlier test's by chance.

Full suite: 111/111 passing. Verified test_duplicate_signed_request_outside_
window_is_rejected catches a regression by removing the _check_not_replayed
call entirely and re-running (fails as expected).
